---
import SimulatorLayout from '../../layouts/SimulatorLayout.astro';
import CellSim from '../../simulators/cell3d/CellSim.astro';
import { cellStructureQuiz } from '../../data/quizzes/cell-structure';

const toc = [
  { id: 'cells', label: 'Cells: the units of life' },
  { id: 'organelles', label: 'The main organelles' },
  { id: 'protein', label: 'A protein’s journey' },
  { id: 'size', label: 'How small is a cell?' },
  { id: 'types', label: 'Prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells' },
  { id: 'terms', label: 'Key terms' },
  { id: 'applications', label: 'Why it matters' },
  { id: 'misconceptions', label: 'Common misconceptions' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  { text: 'Clark, M. A., Douglas, M., & Choi, J. (2018). Biology 2e, Section 4.3: Eukaryotic Cells. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-3-eukaryotic-cells' },
  { text: 'Clark, M. A., Douglas, M., & Choi, J. (2018). Biology 2e, Section 4.4: The Endomembrane System and Proteins. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/4-4-the-endomembrane-system-and-proteins' },
  { text: 'Alberts, B., et al. (2022). Molecular Biology of the Cell (7th ed.). W. W. Norton.' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="biology"
  topic="cell-structure"
  description="Interactive 3D animal cell: rotate a cutaway cell and click the nucleus, mitochondria, ribosomes, endoplasmic reticulum, Golgi apparatus, lysosomes and more to learn what each organelle does."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={cellStructureQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      Every living thing is made of cells, and each cell is a busy factory with specialised compartments called
      organelles. Rotate a 3D animal cell with a slice cut away, click each structure, and follow how they work together
      to keep the cell alive.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <CellSim slot="sim" kind="animal" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Drag</strong> to rotate the cell and use the <strong>+</strong> and <strong>−</strong> buttons to zoom. On a phone, tap the model first.</li>
      <li><strong>Click any organelle</strong> — or choose one from the list — to highlight it and read what it does.</li>
      <li>Switch the <strong>cutaway</strong> off to see the whole cell surface, and turn <strong>labels</strong> on or off.</li>
      <li>Try to explore every organelle: the counter shows how many you have found.</li>
      <li>Keyboard users: Tab to the 3D view and use the arrow keys to rotate. <strong>Reset</strong> restores the starting view.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="cells">Cells: the units of life</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>cell theory</strong>, developed in the 1800s, states that all living things are made of one or more
    cells, that the cell is the basic unit of life, and that new cells come only from existing cells. Animal cells are
    <strong>eukaryotic</strong>: their DNA is enclosed in a nucleus, and they contain membrane-bound organelles, each
    specialised for a particular job — like departments in a factory.
  </p>

  <h2 id="organelles">The main organelles</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Organelle</th><th>Job</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Cell membrane</td><td>Controls what enters and leaves the cell</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Nucleus (with nucleolus)</td><td>Stores DNA and controls the cell; the nucleolus makes ribosome parts</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mitochondria</td><td>Release energy by aerobic respiration</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Ribosomes</td><td>Build proteins</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Rough endoplasmic reticulum</td><td>Folds and transports proteins made by its ribosomes</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Smooth endoplasmic reticulum</td><td>Makes lipids and breaks down toxins</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Golgi apparatus</td><td>Modifies, sorts and packages proteins into vesicles</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Lysosomes</td><td>Digest worn-out organelles and engulfed material</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Centrioles</td><td>Organise the spindle during cell division</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    All of these sit in the <strong>cytoplasm</strong>, a jelly-like fluid where many chemical reactions happen. A
    network of protein fibres, the <strong>cytoskeleton</strong>, gives the cell its shape and moves organelles around.
  </p>

  <h2 id="protein">A protein’s journey</h2>
  <p>Organelles work together. Here is how a cell makes and exports a protein such as a digestive enzyme or hormone:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>The <strong>nucleus</strong> copies the gene into messenger RNA, which leaves through a nuclear pore.</li>
    <li><strong>Ribosomes</strong> on the rough ER read the RNA and build the protein.</li>
    <li>The protein is folded inside the <strong>rough ER</strong> and carried in a vesicle to the <strong>Golgi apparatus</strong>.</li>
    <li>The Golgi modifies and packages it into a new <strong>vesicle</strong>.</li>
    <li>The vesicle fuses with the <strong>cell membrane</strong> and releases the protein outside the cell.</li>
  </ol>
  <p><strong>Mitochondria</strong> supply the energy (ATP) needed at every step.</p>

  <h2 id="size">How small is a cell?</h2>
  <p>
    A typical animal cell is about 10–30 micrometres (µm) across — roughly 1/50 of a millimetre. A mitochondrion is
    about 1 µm long, and a ribosome only about 25 nanometres. That is why cells were not discovered until microscopes
    were invented: Robert Hooke first described “cells” in cork in 1665. Organelles such as ribosomes can only be seen
    with an electron microscope.
  </p>

  <h2 id="types">Prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells</h2>
  <p>
    Bacteria are <strong>prokaryotic</strong>: they are much smaller, have no nucleus (their DNA floats in the
    cytoplasm) and lack membrane-bound organelles, though they do have ribosomes. Animals, plants, fungi and protists are
    made of <strong>eukaryotic</strong> cells like the one in this model.
  </p>

  <h2 id="terms">Key terms</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Organelle</dt>
    <dd>A specialised structure within a cell that performs a particular job.</dd>
    <dt>Cytoplasm</dt>
    <dd>The jelly-like material inside the cell membrane, surrounding the nucleus.</dd>
    <dt>Eukaryotic cell</dt>
    <dd>A cell with a nucleus and membrane-bound organelles.</dd>
    <dt>ATP</dt>
    <dd>Adenosine triphosphate, the molecule that carries energy for cell processes.</dd>
    <dt>Vesicle</dt>
    <dd>A small membrane bubble that transports substances within the cell.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="applications">Why it matters</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Medicine:</strong> many diseases involve faulty organelles — for example, some inherited disorders affect mitochondria or lysosomes (educational context only).</li>
    <li><strong>Antibiotics:</strong> some target bacterial ribosomes, which differ from ours, so they harm bacteria but not human cells.</li>
    <li><strong>Specialised cells:</strong> muscle cells are packed with mitochondria; gland cells have extensive ER and Golgi to make secretions.</li>
    <li><strong>Biotechnology:</strong> cells are used as factories to make insulin and vaccines.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“Cells are flat, like textbook diagrams.”</strong> They are three-dimensional and crowded with organelles.</li>
    <li><strong>“The nucleus is the brain of the cell and does everything.”</strong> Organelles divide the work; the nucleus provides instructions.</li>
    <li><strong>“All cells look the same.”</strong> Cells vary hugely in shape and contents depending on their function.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Animal cells are eukaryotic, with a nucleus and membrane-bound organelles.</li>
    <li>Each organelle has a specific job; together they make, package and export products.</li>
    <li>Mitochondria release energy; ribosomes build proteins; the Golgi packages them.</li>
    <li>A cell’s structure reflects its function.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="muted">Model note: organelles are shown larger and fewer than in a real cell so that each can be seen and selected.</p>
</SimulatorLayout>
